\section{Analysis}
\label{sec:analysis}

\subsection{Symbolic Confirmation Bias}
\label{sec:confirmation-bias}

\looseness=-1 Self-extracted constraints systematically align with the model's majority answer. On Mistral-7B FOLIO, when SICA errs, 83.3\% of constraints support the incorrect answer (bias ratio $4.97\times$; Table~\ref{tab:cross-model-bias}), and 94\% of errors overlap with SC. Z3 rejects only 3.4\% of constraints, and MAX-SAT produces answers identical to count-only aggregation on ${\geq}99\%$ of problems (Table~\ref{tab:alpha-ablation}); the formal verification layer is thus vacuous (Eq.~\ref{eq:degenerate}). A permutation test confirms BR reflects trace-count geometry, not extraction bias (Appendix~\ref{app:br-permutation}); controls rule out answer leakage (Appendix~\ref{app:per-trace-separation}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Symbolic confirmation bias across nine models on FOLIO ($n{=}204$). BR: mean per-problem bias ratio. All models exhibit $\text{BR} > 1$. Ext.: $^a$self-extraction; $^b$cross-model (Mistral-7B extracts FOL from target traces). $^*$Interim ($n{=}95$). $^\dagger$$n{=}196$ (8 API failures excluded). $^\ddagger$$n{=}161$ (43 API failures excluded).}
\label{tab:frontier-scb}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{\begin{tabular}{@{}llrrrr@{}}
\toprule
Model & Ext. & SC (\%) & $n_\text{err}$ & BR & $\Delta$ (pp) \\
\midrule
Mistral-7B & $^a$ & 55.88 & 90 & 4.97 & 0.00 \\
LLaMA-3.1-8B & $^a$ & --- & --- & 3.87 & --- \\
Qwen2.5-14B & $^a$ & 75.98 & 49 & 3.93 & $-$0.49 \\
\addlinespace
R1-Distill-8B & $^b$ & 74.51 & 52 & 2.92 & $+$2.45 \\
gpt-4o$^\ddagger$ & $^a$ & 73.91 & 42 & 2.94 & $-$1.86 \\
gpt-4.1 & $^b$ & 82.84 & 35 & 2.70 & $-$1.47 \\
gpt-5.5 & $^b$ & 84.31 & 32 & 3.48 & $+$0.49 \\
o3$^\dagger$ & $^b$ & 82.65 & 34 & 1.66 & 0.00 \\
Gemini-2.5-Pro$^*$ & $^b$ & 87.37 & 12 & 2.79 & $-$1.05 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\looseness=-1 The pattern generalizes beyond locally-run models. Table~\ref{tab:frontier-scb} reports SCB across nine models spanning 7B open-source to frontier closed-source, all exhibiting $\text{BR} > 1$ ($1.66$--$4.97\times$). Open-source models use self-extraction; frontier models use cross-model extraction (Mistral-7B extracts FOL from target traces). The bias also holds across all cells of a $2{\times}2$ generator${\times}$extractor matrix (Appendix~\ref{app:2x2}).

\smallskip\noindent
\textbf{Observation 1} \textit{(Argmax Preservation).}\;%
Let $t_1, \ldots, t_K \overset{\mathrm{i.i.d.}}{\sim} P_M(\cdot \mid q)$ with trace-determined answers $a_i = h(t_i)$ and $n_a = |\{i : a_i = a\}|$.
Under (i)~the degeneration condition $|\mathcal{C}^{\setminus}| \approx 0$ (Eq.~\ref{eq:degenerate}), (ii)~source-attribution scoring where $\sigma(c,a) {=} 1$ iff $c$ originates from a trace producing $a$ (Eq.~\ref{eq:score}), and (iii)~approximately uniform constraint yield per trace, SICA reduces to majority vote: $\arg\max_a \operatorname{score}(a) = \arg\max_a n_a$.

\smallskip\noindent
\textit{Empirical support.}\;%
Z3 rejection rate $|\mathcal{C}^{\setminus}|/|\mathcal{C}| \approx 0.034$ confirms $|\mathcal{C}^{\setminus}| \approx 0$. Per-trace yield averages $5.6$--$6.0$ constraints across answer groups (CV${=}0.26$; Appendix~\ref{app:yield-cv}); weight is thus proportional to $n_a$. SICA and majority vote agree on 96.1\% of problems for Qwen2.5-14B ($n{=}804$; Table~\ref{tab:ablation}) and over 90\% across all conditions. Per-trace separation analysis confirms the extraction function is neutral (constraint density and quality are balanced across answer groups), and source-attribution scoring is the sole mechanism transmitting CoT correlation into verification (Appendix~\ref{app:per-trace-separation}).

\looseness=-1 A representative discordant case illustrates the mechanism. On a ProofWriter chaining problem (White$\to$Round$\to$Young), SC correctly breaks a 6:6 vote tie to the gold label. SICA extracts 17 constraints; MAX-SAT excludes 2 that capture correct intermediate derivations present \emph{only} in correct-answer traces, penalizing the complete reasoning chain and selecting the wrong answer. Of 31 discordant problems ($n{=}804$), all occur at exact or near vote ties (entropy $\geq 0.92$), and constraint exclusion tips the balance in either direction with no systematic corrective tendency (Appendix~\ref{app:case-studies}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\columnwidth]{figures/fig3_process_kappa_scatter.pdf}
\caption{Answer agreement vs.\ CoT cosine similarity (Mistral-7B, FOLIO, $n{=}204$). Flat regression ($r = -0.06$, $p = 0.36$): process and answer independence are uncorrelated.}
\label{fig:process-kappa}
\end{figure}

\label{sec:process-kappa}
\looseness=-1 The $M{\to}T{\to}(A,C)$ generation structure provides empirical motivation \citep{cover2006elements}: under degeneration (Observation~1), constraints carry no more task-relevant information than answers alone, i.e., $I(C;\, Y) \leq I(A;\, Y)$ (Figure~\ref{fig:causal-dag}). Process-level overlap compounds the bottleneck: mean reasoning similarity reaches $0.69$, far exceeding answer-level $\kappa$ ($0.41$--$0.51$; Figure~\ref{fig:process-kappa}), and SICA gain decreases monotonically with process similarity ($\rho = -1.0$, $p = 0.0083$; Appendix~\ref{app:cil}).

\subsection{Why Remediation Fails}
\label{sec:remediation}
\label{sec:interventions}

\looseness=-1 All 27 training-free variants spanning four categories produce neutral-to-negative results (Figure~\ref{fig:kappa-accuracy}; Appendices~\ref{app:extraction}--\ref{app:entropy}); a persistent independence--accuracy trade-off emerges. Diversity-seeking strategies shift $K_\text{eff}$ by $< 0.5$; constraint-level interventions leave degeneration intact; debate ($\kappa = 0.15$, $\Delta = -18.63$\,pp) breaks correlation but collapses accuracy (Appendix~\ref{app:error-categorization}); cross-model extraction achieves the lowest non-destructive $\kappa$ (${\approx}0.40$--$0.47$) but remains above the independence threshold ($+0.49$\,pp, n.s.; Appendix~\ref{app:intervention-individual}).

\looseness=-1 The root cause is \emph{variable isolation}: each trace generates constraints with its own variable names, so Z3 treats identically-intended propositions from different traces as unrelated symbols. Among cross-answer constraint pairs, 56.4\% share zero Z3 variables, making them trivially jointly satisfiable. Consequently, although 91.8\% of constraints are technically discriminative (producing different satisfiability outcomes for different candidate answers), this discrimination merely mirrors the model's answer distribution rather than providing independent corrective signal (Appendix~\ref{app:extraction}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-generator NLI escape with DeBERTa-large-mnli verifier (55.00\% standalone, ProofWriter $n{=}600$). Combo gain decreases monotonically with generator capability.}
\label{tab:cross_generator_nli}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{\begin{tabular}{@{}llcccc@{}}
\toprule
Generator & Family & SC & Gap & Combo $\Delta$ ($w{=}3$) & $p$ \\
\midrule
Mistral-7B & Mistral & 39.33\% & $+$15.67 & $+$6.67\,pp & $<$0.001 \\
LLaMA-3.1-8B & LLaMA & 72.50\% & $-$17.50 & $+$0.83\,pp & 0.665 \\
Qwen2.5-14B & Qwen & 74.00\% & $-$19.00 & $+$0.67\,pp & 0.503 \\
Qwen3-14B & Qwen & 85.83\% & $-$30.83 & $+$0.50\,pp & 0.453 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\subsection{Diagnostic Framework}
\label{sec:diagnostic-framework}
\label{sec:diagnostics}

\looseness=-1 Three lightweight diagnostics predict the outcome of any self-extraction-based aggregation from a $K{=}12$ pilot run: (1)~degenerate-regime indicator $C_0 < 5\%$ (SICA reduces to SC); (2)~diversity floor $K_\text{eff} \leq 1.30$ (insufficient vote diversity); (3)~bias ratio BR combined with SC regime. These boundaries correctly characterize failure modes across all 60 tested conditions within the self-extraction paradigm (Appendix~\ref{app:failure-modes}).

\looseness=-1 Three distinct failure modes correspond to these regimes. \textbf{Type~A} (SC $\geq 60\%$): strong models produce consistent traces whose constraints mirror the answer distribution; MAX-SAT excludes few clauses ($|\mathcal{C}^{\setminus}| \approx 0$) and scoring degenerates to weighted vote counting (Eq.~\ref{eq:degenerate}). \textbf{Type~C} (SC $\leq 40\%$): the majority of traces reach incorrect answers; constraints extracted from wrong traces encode incorrect reasoning as formal rules, and Z3 preferentially satisfies these wrong constraints, amplifying errors ($-2.83$\,pp for Mistral-7B on ProofWriter). An intermediate band (40--60\% SC) contains the only positive trends, but none reach significance. Entropy decomposition confirms this structure: on low-entropy problems (majority count $\geq 9/12$), $\Delta \approx 0$\,pp uniformly---constraints carry no information beyond the majority vote. On high-entropy problems (majority $\leq 8$), the effect direction depends on the SC regime: intermediate-SC yields $\Delta$ up to $+12.8$\,pp, while Type~A and Type~C produce negative effects ($-8.2$ and $-4.9$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:entropy}).

\looseness=-1 Decision boundaries were derived from Mistral-7B and Qwen2.5-14B and validated prospectively on held-out generators (LLaMA-3.1-8B, Qwen3-14B), correctly predicting Type~A bias ($\Delta = -4.41$\,pp) and non-significance ($\Delta \leq +1.47$\,pp). Conversely, when diagnostics detect sufficient capability gap (\S\ref{sec:cross-arch}), the framework correctly predicts positive gain ($+6.67$\,pp for Mistral-7B on ProofWriter). The three diagnostics thus enable practitioners to rule out self-extraction pipelines before full-scale deployment (Appendix~\ref{app:diagnostic-toolkit}).

\subsection{Cross-Architecture Escape}
\label{sec:cross-arch}

\looseness=-1 No training-free self-extraction method escapes the distributional prior (\S\ref{sec:remediation}). We test whether a structurally independent verifier can break the bottleneck using DeBERTa-large \citep{he2021deberta} (406M, encoder-only), fine-tuned on MultiNLI \citep{williams2018mnli} for natural language inference (NLI). Each NLI-supporting vote counts $w$ times alongside SC's $K$ votes.

\looseness=-1 DeBERTa alone achieves 49.51\% on FOLIO and 55.00\% on ProofWriter. Combo $w{=}3$ yields $+6.67$\,pp on ProofWriter for Mistral-7B ($p < 0.001$, significant after BH correction) and $+2.94$\,pp on FOLIO (n.s.); all generators with SC $\geq 72\%$ show no significant improvement ($\Delta \leq +1.47$\,pp). Same-model SICA \emph{degrades} ProofWriter ($-2.83$\,pp; Table~\ref{tab:main_results}); corrective signal must therefore originate outside the model's distribution. The escape generalizes across NLI architectures: three verifiers (DeBERTa, RoBERTa \citep{liu2019roberta}, BART \citep{lewis2020bart}, all fine-tuned on MultiNLI) produce significant gains at $w{=}3$ ($+6.67$, $+4.17$, $+5.17$\,pp; 10 of 15 verifier${\times}$weight conditions survive BH correction; Appendix~\ref{app:multi-nli}), confirming that structural independence, not model-specific properties, drives the effect.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\columnwidth]{figures/capability_gap_scatter}
\caption{Combo gain vs.\ capability gap (verifier standalone $-$ generator SC) at $w{=}3$ across three NLI architectures. Significant gains occur only when the generator falls below the verifier's standalone accuracy. Stars: domain-adapted DeBERTa (Appendix~\ref{app:finetuned-nli}).}
\label{fig:capability-gap}
\end{figure}

\looseness=-1 Across 108 conditions, gain decreases monotonically with generator accuracy (Table~\ref{tab:cross_generator_nli}; Figure~\ref{fig:capability-gap}), a preliminary \emph{capability-gap pattern} ($\rho = 1.0$, $p = 0.003$; exploratory, $n{=}6$). Fine-tuning extends gains to generators with SC up to ${\sim}$72\% (Appendix~\ref{app:finetuned-nli}). Cross-model LLM voting degrades strong generators while NLI never does; structural independence drives the escape (Appendices~\ref{app:cross-model-ablation},~\ref{app:drc}).

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}
\label{sec:pipeline}
\label{sec:condorcet}
\label{sec:effective-k}

Self-Consistency (SC) \citep{wang2023sc} samples $K$ reasoning traces and selects the majority answer. Under correlated traces (Fleiss' $\kappa$ \citep{fleiss1971kappa}), the effective ensemble size $K_\text{eff}$ is
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:effk}
K_{\text{eff}} = K \,/\, [1 + (K{-}1) \cdot \kappa]
\end{equation}
(following \citet{ladha1992correlated}). Intuitively, $K_\text{eff}$ measures how many effectively independent votes $K$ correlated traces contribute; e.g., $K{=}12$ traces with $\kappa{=}0.69$ yield $K_\text{eff} \approx 1.4$, meaning twelve correlated traces carry no more independent information than 1.4 uncorrelated ones. This motivates constraint-based aggregation.

Symbolic Invariant Consensus Aggregation (SICA) extends SC by extracting formal constraints from traces and selecting the answer best supported by the satisfiable subset (Figure~\ref{fig:pipeline}).

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{SICA vs.\ SC across all non-degenerate self-extraction conditions (sorted by SC). Two conditions show significant degradation after BH correction ($m{=}6$ confirmatory; remaining exploratory); no condition shows a significant gain. $\Delta$ = SICA $-$ SC (pp). Symbols: $^b$gold ICL; \textbf{bold}: $p < 0.05$; {*}\,BH adj.\ $p = 0.042$; $^{3s}$\,3-seed aggregate; $^{4s}$\,4-seed confirmatory aggregate; $^\dagger$\,4-seed replication in Table~\ref{tab:multi-seed} (unmarked: single-seed exploratory; s123/s456/s789 are individual seed runs). All conditions have McNemar power $< 80\%$ for $|\Delta| = 2$\,pp (Appendix~\ref{app:power}). Degenerate conditions ($C_0 < 5\%$: QwQ-32B AIME$^\ddagger$ $n{=}22$, Qwen2.5-14B GSM8K$^\ddagger$ $n{=}30$) yield $\Delta = 0$ and are reported separately in Appendix~\ref{app:oracle}. $^\ddagger$Pilot conditions ($n{\leq}30$); included for coverage, not confirmatory evidence. Single-seed conditions use seed 123 (not selected post hoc).}
\label{tab:main_results}
\begin{tabular}{@{}llrrrrl@{}}
\toprule
Model & Domain & $n$ & SC (\%) & SICA (\%) & $\Delta$ (pp) & $p$ \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{7}{l}{\textit{Type C: error amplification (SC $\leq$ 40\%)}} \\
Mistral-7B & PW-D5 & 600 & 39.33 & 36.50 & $\mathbf{-2.83}$ & 0.014{*} \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{7}{l}{\textit{Intermediate SC (SC $\sim$50--60\%, Mistral-7B only)}} \\
Mistral-7B & LogiQA & 200 & 50.00 & 51.50 & $+$1.50 & 0.581 \\
Mistral-7B & FOLIO (s123) & 204 & 54.41 & 57.35 & $+$2.94 & 0.070 \\
Mistral-7B$^{3s}$ & FOLIO & 204 & 56.53 & 56.70 & $+$0.16 & 1.000 \\
Mistral-7B & FOLIO (s456) & 204 & 55.88 & 55.88 & 0.00 & 1.000 \\
Mistral-7B & FOLIO (s789) & 204 & 59.31 & 56.86 & $-$2.45 & 0.267 \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{7}{l}{\textit{Type A: confirmation bias (SC $\geq$ 60\%)}} \\
LLaMA-3.1-8B & FOLIO & 204 & 66.18 & 61.76 & $-$4.41 & 0.093 \\
LLaMA-3.1-8B & StrategyQA & 200 & 72.00 & 74.00 & $+$2.00 & 0.424 \\
\addlinespace
Qwen2.5-14B & FOLIO (ICL)$^b$ & 199 & 74.37 & 74.87 & $+$0.50 & 1.000 \\
Qwen2.5-14B & PW-D5 & 600 & 74.00 & 71.50 & $\mathbf{-2.50}$ & 0.011{*} \\
Qwen2.5-14B$^{4s}$ & FOLIO & 204 & 75.01 & 73.78 & $-$1.24 & 1.000 \\
Qwen2.5-7B & FOLIO & 204 & 75.98 & 76.47 & $+$0.49 & 1.000 \\
Qwen2.5-14B & LogiQA & 200 & 79.00 & 78.00 & $-$1.00 & 0.625 \\
\addlinespace
Qwen3-14B & FOLIO & 204 & 83.33 & 84.80 & $+$1.47 & 1.000 \\
Qwen3-14B$^\dagger$ & PW-D5 & 600 & 85.83 & 85.67 & $-$0.17 & 1.000 \\
Qwen3-14B (think) & FOLIO & 204 & 86.27 & 86.76 & $+$0.49 & 1.000 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

\paragraph{Trace generation.} Given question $q$, an LLM generates $K$ traces $\{t_1, \ldots, t_K\}$ at temperature $T = 0.7$. Each trace $t_i$ yields answer $a_i$.

\looseness=-1
\paragraph{Constraint extraction.}\label{sec:constraint-extraction} The same LLM extracts logical constraints from each trace at $T_{\text{ext}} = 0.3$. The extraction prompt requests JSON output with constraint type, expression, and Z3-compatible formula (Appendix~\ref{app:prompts}); in practice, 99.0\% of extracted constraints are propositional at the Z3 level, with only 1.0\% using true quantifiers. The \emph{extraction rate} is the fraction of traces yielding at least one parseable constraint.

\paragraph{Deduplication and MAX-SAT.} Constraints are deduplicated by syntactic equivalence (global average deduplication ratio: 46.0\%); each unique constraint $c_m$ receives weight $w_m$ equal to the number of source traces. The objective finds the largest-weight satisfiable subset:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:maxsat}
\mathcal{C}^* = \arg\max_{\mathcal{S} \subseteq \mathcal{C}} \sum_{c \in \mathcal{S}} w_c \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \mathcal{S} \text{ is satisfiable.}
\end{equation}
Z3 \citep{demoura2008z3} solves this with a 10-second timeout (Algorithm~\ref{alg:maxsat}; Appendix~\ref{app:z3-pseudocode}). Answer assertion constraints ($\texttt{answer\_a} == \texttt{True}$, weight 1) ensure every candidate receives a nonzero base score; SICA is thus a strict generalization of SC.

\paragraph{Scoring.} Let $\mathcal{C}^{\setminus} = \mathcal{C} \setminus \mathcal{C}^*$. Each candidate $a$ is scored by:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:score}
\text{score}(a) = \sum_{c \in \mathcal{C}^*} w_c \cdot \sigma(c, a) - \alpha \sum_{c \in \mathcal{C}^{\setminus}} w_c \cdot \sigma(c, a),
\end{equation}
where $\sigma(c, a) = 1$ if $c$ was extracted from any trace producing answer $a$, and $\alpha = 0.5$. The selected answer is $a^* = \arg\max_{a} \text{score}(a)$.

\paragraph{Degeneration condition.} If self-extracted constraints are coupled to their source answer, MAX-SAT retains nearly all ($|\mathcal{C}^{\setminus}| \approx 0$), and scoring degenerates to:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:degenerate}
\text{score}(a) \approx \sum_{\substack{c \in \mathcal{C}^* \\ \text{source}(c) \to a}} w_c,
\end{equation}
equivalent to majority voting. We show in \S\ref{sec:confirmation-bias} that this degeneration occurs in practice.

\paragraph{The $C_0$ diagnostic.} The multi-candidate rate $C_0 = |\{q : |\mathcal{A}(q)| > 1\}| / |\mathcal{Q}|$ is a lightweight diagnostic computable from pilot traces: $C_0 < 5\%$ means SICA reduces to SC. $\alpha = 0.5$ is not critical (${\pm}0.49$\,pp across $\alpha \in [0, 1]$).

\paragraph{Bias ratio.} We quantify extraction bias via the \emph{bias ratio} (BR):
\begin{equation}\label{eq:mirage}
\text{BR}(M,T) = \frac{P(c \text{ supports } a_M \mid a_M \neq a^*)}{P(c \text{ supports } a^* \mid a_M \neq a^*)},
\end{equation}
where $c$ is a self-extracted constraint, $a_M$ the model's majority answer, and $a^*$ the ground truth. BR quantifies the degree of extraction degeneration under source-attribution scoring (Observation~1): values above 1 reflect the trace-count base rate inherent to source-attributed constraints. $\text{BR} = 1$ indicates no coupling beyond chance. We validate this metric empirically in \S\ref{sec:confirmation-bias}.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related-work}

\looseness=-1
\paragraph{Self-consistency and ensemble theory.}
Self-consistency (SC) selects the majority answer across chain-of-thought traces \citep{wang2023sc}, with extensions for free-form generation \citep{chen2024usc}, confidence weighting \citep{taubenfeld2025cisc}, diverse prompting \citep{li2023diverse}, and reasoning-aware filtering \citep{wan2024rasc, toh2024notallvotes, kim2026reasc}. SC gains plateau early \citep{loo2025reevaluating, byerly2025selfconsistency}. The Condorcet Jury Theorem \citep{condorcet1785} guarantees majority-vote convergence under independence; ensemble theory \citep{dietterich2000ensemble, krogh1994neural} requires error diversity. \citet{ladha1992correlated} show correlated voters reduce effective ensemble size; our $K_\text{eff}$ adapts Fleiss' $\kappa$ from diversity metrics \citep{kuncheva2003measures, brown2005diversity} to quantify this (\S\ref{sec:pipeline}). \citet{kim2025correlated} demonstrate correlated LLM errors; \citet{kuai2026independent} propose cross-model independence auditing.

\looseness=-1
\paragraph{Neuro-symbolic reasoning.}
Three paradigms exist: \emph{specification-generation} \citep{pan2023logiclm, olausson2023linc, ye2024satlm} and \emph{program-generation} \citep{gao2023pal, chen2023program, lyu2023faithful} formalize before answer generation; \emph{trace-extraction} (this work) operates post-hoc, inheriting confirmation bias (\S\ref{sec:confirmation-bias}). Recent work extends symbolic planning and verified reasoning \citep{ryu2025clover, xu2026dylos, xu2025aristotle, pei2025fover, singh2026verge, luo2025logicagent, miya2025eidoku}; \citet{amonkar2025realitycheck} show formalization quality degrades with complexity; \citet{chan2026position} argue logical soundness alone is unreliable. Prior studies note high formalization error rates but treat them as quality problems; we show they are \emph{systematically biased} toward the model's answer (Observation~1). This reveals a mechanism underlying \citet{huang2024selfcorrect}'s finding that LLMs cannot self-correct without external feedback: we formalize the failure via the $M{\to}T{\to}(A,C)$ generation structure and provide three lightweight diagnostics that predict failure modes before deployment. Our symbolic confirmation bias (SCB) is distinct from CoT-level confirmation bias \citep{wan2025unveiling, turpin2023language, jhaveri2026failing}: CoT bias concerns trace-level faithfulness, while SCB shows the \emph{collection} of self-extracted constraints mirrors the answer distribution even when individual traces are locally faithful.

\looseness=-1
\paragraph{Verification and test-time scaling.}
Scaling test-time compute via additional traces \citep{snell2025scaling}, structured search \citep{yao2023tree}, or extended reasoning \citep{guo2025deepseekr1, qwen2024qwq} faces the same correlation challenge when traces share a model's biases. Self-verification methods, including multi-agent debate \citep{liang2023encouraging, du2023improving}, self-evaluation \citep{xie2023selfevaluation, weng2023selfverification, madaan2023selfrefine, dhuliawala2023chainofverification}, iterative improvement \citep{shinn2023reflexion, zhou2023lats}, tool-augmented critique \citep{gou2024critic}, formal CoT verification \citep{feng2025vericot, chen2025sets}, and multi-agent verification \citep{lifshitz2025mav}, report gains but none tests self-extracted constraints under formal solvers \citep[cf.][]{kamoi2024selfcorrect}; the bottleneck lies in constraint extraction, not verification (\S\ref{sec:confirmation-bias}). Trained verifiers can escape this bottleneck through external reward signals: outcome and process reward models \citep{cobbe2021gsm8k, lightman2024prm, wang2024mathshepherd}, discriminator-guided decoding \citep{khalifa2023grace}, RL-based self-verification \citep{zeng2025s2r}, dual preference optimization \citep{dupo2025}, and execution-based filtering \citep{li2022alphacode, chen2023codet}. These operate outside Observation~1's pipeline and may overcome the information-theoretic bottleneck. The failure modes differ: SICA's bottleneck is extraction fidelity, whereas PRM/ORM's is reward model generalization \citep{wang2024mathshepherd}.

